select 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-000000000028', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Criminology is best described as...","options":["the practice of prosecuting offenders in court","the scientific study of crime, its causes, and society''s response to it","a branch of medicine that treats injured victims","a method for designing prison buildings"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Criminology studies crime, offenders, and how societies prevent and respond to crime - it is a social science, not a courtroom role."},{"prompt":"The ''dark figure of crime'' refers to...","options":["crimes committed only after dark","the gap between crimes that occur and crimes recorded by officials","the most serious category of felony","the total financial cost of crime"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The dark figure is the large volume of offenses that never reach official records, often because they are unreported."},{"prompt":"The classical school, linked to Beccaria and Bentham, assumes offenders are...","options":["born criminals who cannot change","rational agents who weigh the costs and benefits of an act","controlled entirely by their surroundings","acting purely at random"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Classical thinking treats crime as a free, calculated choice, which is why it stresses deterrence."},{"prompt":"Robert Merton''s strain theory holds that crime can arise when...","options":["people inherit criminal genes","a society promotes goals like wealth but blocks legitimate means to reach them","police wrongly label the innocent","offenders have no contact with other offenders"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Strain theory locates one route to crime in the mismatch between cultural goals and available legitimate means."},{"prompt":"Edwin Sutherland''s differential association theory claims criminal behavior is mainly...","options":["inherited biologically","learned through interaction with others","a result of low intelligence","a purely economic calculation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Differential association says people learn both techniques and pro-crime attitudes from close groups."},{"prompt":"Labeling theory is chiefly concerned with...","options":["the chemical labels on controlled substances","how being officially defined as deviant can shape a person''s later behavior","the ranking of crimes by severity","the branding used by organized crime"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Labeling theory studies how societal reaction and the label itself can push a person toward further offending."},{"prompt":"The three core components of a criminal justice system are usually described as...","options":["victims, offenders, and witnesses","police, courts, and corrections","laws, lawyers, and judges","arrest, trial, and appeal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Most systems are organized as three linked stages: police, courts, and corrections."},{"prompt":"A central aim of victimology is to...","options":["defend offenders at trial","study the experiences, needs, and role of victims","set mandatory minimum sentences","audit police budgets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Victimology studies victims - who is harmed, the consequences, and how systems can treat them fairly."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'What criminology is, and how crime is defined and measured', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain what criminology is as a discipline, distinguish crime from deviance, and describe the main ways crime is counted. By the end you will be able to say why two reasonable people can look at the same year and disagree about whether crime rose or fell, and why the figures we rely on are always partial. Warm-up from the diagnostic: what does the dark figure of crime describe? We will build on that idea throughout. Criminology sits at the crossroads of sociology, law, psychology, and statistics, so part of the skill is noticing which lens a given claim is using. You will also learn to treat a crime rate not as a simple fact about the world but as the product of choices: what the law counts as a crime, whether victims report it, and whether officials record it. Holding those choices in view is the foundation for everything that follows, from testing theories to evaluating policy. For any figure you meet, keep asking where it came from and what it leaves out."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Criminology is the scientific study of crime, the people who commit it, and the ways societies try to prevent and respond to it. Crime is behavior the law prohibits and punishes; deviance is any behavior that breaks social norms, which is broader and shifts across time and place. Not all deviance is criminal, and not every harmful act is against the law. Measuring crime is harder than it sounds, and three main sources each have blind spots. Police-recorded statistics count only crimes that are reported and then written down, so they partly measure police activity. Victimization surveys ask representative samples what has happened to them, capturing offenses never reported to police. Self-report studies ask people to admit offenses they committed. The dark figure of crime is the large volume of offenses that never appear in official records. Because reporting and recording practices change, a rising recorded rate can reflect better reporting rather than more crime. Good criminology questions every number, triangulating across sources rather than trusting any single count."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Early in the nineteenth century, Adolphe Quetelet used the first national crime statistics to show that recorded crime was surprisingly stable from year to year, suggesting social patterns rather than random individual acts. Emile Durkheim later argued that crime is a normal feature of every society and can even reinforce shared values by marking the boundaries of acceptable behavior. Edwin Sutherland offered a durable definition of criminology as the study of lawmaking, lawbreaking, and the response to lawbreaking - a reminder that the field studies the whole system, not just offenders. For a concrete example of the dark figure, compare sources on sexual assault: victimization surveys routinely record far more incidents than police data, because many victims never report. The same gap appears for minor theft and domestic violence. Another example: when a police force changes how it records a category, the recorded rate can jump without any real change in behavior. These cases show why criminologists cross-check police records, surveys, and self-reports before drawing any conclusion."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Try these, then check yourself. 1) In one sentence, how does crime differ from deviance? 2) Name the three main sources of crime data. 3) Which source is best placed to capture the dark figure of crime? 4) Give one reason a recorded crime rate might rise even if offending did not. 5) Why did Durkheim call crime normal? Answers: 1) Crime breaks the law and is punishable, while deviance breaks social norms more broadly and need not be illegal. 2) Police-recorded statistics, victimization surveys, and self-report studies. 3) Victimization surveys, because they reach people who never contacted the police, though self-report studies also help. 4) Improved reporting by victims, a recording rule change, or more proactive policing can each lift the count without more crime. 5) Because it appears in every known society and helps mark the limits of acceptable conduct, so its presence is expected rather than a sign of collapse."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating police-recorded figures as a complete census of crime - they miss everything in the dark figure. Mistake: assuming a higher recorded rate always means more offending, when it may mean better reporting or a recording change. Mistake: equating deviance with crime, which erases the many norm-breaking acts that are perfectly legal. Mistake: reading one year in isolation, when trends need several years and more than one source before they mean much. Exit ticket: (a) Which data source asks representative samples whether they have been victims? (b) True or false: all deviant behavior is criminal. (c) What name do we give the crimes that never reach official records? Answers: (a) victimization surveys; (b) false - deviance is broader than crime, and much of it is lawful; (c) the dark figure of crime."}]}'::jsonb, 'Keep crime and deviance distinct: crime breaks the law and is punishable, while deviance breaks social norms more broadly and need not be illegal.', 13),
  (3, 'The classical school and deterrence', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe the classical school of criminology and explain the logic of deterrence. You will be able to summarize what Cesare Beccaria and Jeremy Bentham argued, state the three features that make punishment deter, and say what the evidence suggests about which feature matters most. Warm-up from the diagnostic: the classical school treats offenders as what kind of decision-maker? We will unpack that assumption and test it. By the end you should be able to take a proposed policy, such as a longer prison term, and reason about whether classical theory predicts it will reduce crime, and whether real studies back that prediction. You will also see how these eighteenth-century ideas still shape modern debates about sentencing, policing, and the design of penalties. The goal is not to accept or reject deterrence wholesale but to apply it carefully: to ask when rational-choice reasoning fits offenders well and when it fits poorly, as in crimes of passion or acts committed under the influence of substances."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The classical school, born in the Enlightenment, treats people as rational agents who freely choose their actions by weighing expected pleasure against expected pain. On this view, crime is a choice, and the job of the law is to make the cost of offending outweigh its rewards. Deterrence is the central mechanism. General deterrence aims to discourage the wider public by making an example of punishment; specific deterrence aims to discourage the individual already punished. Classical thinkers argued that punishment works best when it is certain, swift, and proportionate rather than merely harsh. A savage penalty that is rarely applied deters poorly, because a rational offender who expects to escape detection discounts it. Modern research broadly supports this emphasis: the certainty of being caught deters more reliably than the severity of the sentence, and extremely long sentences show weak marginal deterrent effects. The classical model also underpins the principle that penalties should be set in advance, applied equally, and no greater than needed to prevent harm - ideas now embedded in the rule of law."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Cesare Beccaria, in his 1764 essay On Crimes and Punishments, argued against torture and the death penalty and for penalties that are public, prompt, proportionate, and defined by law in advance. His work influenced legal reform across Europe and the framing of modern criminal codes. Jeremy Bentham added the idea of utilitarian calculation: people pursue pleasure and avoid pain, so a well-designed penalty is one just painful enough to outweigh the gain from the crime. Bentham also sketched the panopticon, a prison designed so inmates could always be watched, capturing his belief that the certainty of observation changes behavior. For a modern example, studies of policing find that visibly raising the chance of detection, such as hot-spot patrols, tends to reduce crime more than lengthening sentences does. A contrasting example: crimes committed in rage or deep intoxication often ignore consequences entirely, which is where the rational-choice assumption strains. These cases show both the reach and the limits of the classical model."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Work through these, then check. 1) What core assumption does the classical school make about offenders? 2) Name the three features that make punishment deter. 3) Which feature does modern evidence suggest matters most? 4) Distinguish general from specific deterrence. 5) Give one type of crime where rational-choice reasoning fits poorly. Answers: 1) That they are rational agents who freely weigh the costs and benefits of an act. 2) Certainty, swiftness, and severity (proportionality). 3) Certainty - the probability of being caught - deters more reliably than severity. 4) General deterrence discourages the public at large by example; specific deterrence discourages the particular person who was punished. 5) Crimes of passion, or offenses committed under heavy intoxication, where offenders do not calculate consequences."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming harsher always means more deterrent - the evidence points to certainty of detection over sheer severity. Mistake: thinking classical theory claims everyone calculates perfectly; it is a model that fits some crimes far better than others. Mistake: confusing retribution, which is punishment deserved for a past act, with deterrence, which aims at preventing future acts; a sentence can pursue either or both. Exit ticket: (a) Who wrote On Crimes and Punishments? (b) General or specific deterrence: making an example to discourage the public? (c) True or false: ever-longer sentences reliably produce large extra deterrent effects. Answers: (a) Cesare Beccaria; (b) general deterrence; (c) false - the marginal deterrent effect of lengthening already long sentences is weak."}]}'::jsonb, 'Deterrence rests on three features of punishment - its certainty, its swiftness, and its severity - and the evidence suggests certainty matters most.', 13),
  (4, 'Biological and psychological theories', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Summarize how biological and psychological theories try to explain offending, and judge them critically. You will be able to describe early biological claims and why they were discredited, outline what contemporary research cautiously suggests, and explain the major psychological accounts of criminal behavior. Warm-up: why is it risky to say a single gene or brain feature causes crime? Keep that caution in mind throughout. By the end you should be able to read a headline claiming scientists found the cause of violence and spot the overreach, while still taking seriously the modest, replicated findings about how biology and environment interact. You will also be able to place these individual-level theories alongside the social theories that follow, seeing them as partial rather than competing total explanations. The skill here is balance: rejecting crude biological determinism and its ugly history, without swinging to the opposite error of pretending that bodies, brains, and development play no role at all in behavior."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Biological theories look for factors inside the individual - genetics, brain function, hormones, nutrition, or exposure to toxins - that may raise the risk of offending. Early versions were crude and dangerous: they claimed criminals were a distinct biological type, and such thinking fed discredited and harmful programs. Modern biosocial research is far more careful. It does not claim a crime gene; instead it studies how biological risk factors interact with environment, so that childhood adversity, for example, may matter more for people with certain predispositions. The findings are probabilistic and small, never destiny. Psychological theories focus on personality, learning, and development. Some stress traits such as impulsivity or low self-control; others, drawing on behaviorism, see criminal acts as learned through reward and punishment. Developmental and life-course approaches trace how behavior changes from childhood to adulthood, noting that most offending declines with age. Psychodynamic accounts, following Freud, emphasize early relationships. Across all of these, the responsible conclusion is interaction: biology and psychology shape tendencies that social conditions then amplify or restrain."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Cesare Lombroso, a nineteenth-century physician, argued that criminals were evolutionary throwbacks identifiable by physical features. His method was deeply flawed and his conclusions are rejected today, though he did help push the field toward the empirical study of offenders. Hans Eysenck later linked personality dimensions such as extraversion and neuroticism to offending through differences in how people condition to punishment. Psychologists studying self-control, notably Michael Gottfredson and Travis Hirschi, argued that low self-control formed early in life predicts a wide range of offending. Developmental researchers such as Terrie Moffitt distinguished the small group whose antisocial behavior persists across life from the larger group whose offending is limited to adolescence. For a cautionary example, twin and adoption studies suggest genetics contributes to the risk of antisocial behavior, yet the same studies show environment matters at least as much, which is why no serious researcher reads these results as biological destiny."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Attempt these, then check. 1) Why are Lombroso''s claims rejected today? 2) What does biosocial research mean by interaction? 3) Which psychological trait is central to Gottfredson and Hirschi''s account? 4) What distinction did Moffitt draw among offenders? 5) Why is a correlation between a biological trait and crime not proof of cause? Answers: 1) His physical typing of criminals was methodologically unsound and unsupported, and the ideas fed harmful policies. 2) That biological risk factors raise offending only in combination with particular environments, not on their own. 3) Low self-control, formed early in life. 4) Between life-course-persistent offenders and the larger adolescence-limited group. 5) Because an unmeasured factor, such as poverty or adversity, could drive both, or the causal arrow could run the other way."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: biological determinism - the claim that genes or brains fix who becomes a criminal, which both the evidence and the historical record refute. Mistake: the opposite error of denying any biological or psychological role at all. Mistake: treating a small, probabilistic finding as if it predicted individuals with certainty. Exit ticket: (a) Name one early, discredited biological theorist. (b) Fill in: modern biosocial work stresses the ... of biology and environment. (c) True or false: most adolescent offending continues into adulthood. Answers: (a) Cesare Lombroso; (b) interaction; (c) false - most offending declines with age, and for many people it is limited to adolescence."}]}'::jsonb, 'Correlation is not causation - a trait linked to offending in data does not, by itself, prove the trait causes crime.', 14),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The dark figure of crime is best captured by...","options":["police-recorded statistics","victimization surveys","court conviction records","prison population counts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Victimization surveys reach people who never reported to police, so they catch much of the dark figure."},{"prompt":"According to the classical school, the feature of punishment that deters most reliably is its...","options":["severity","certainty","publicity","cost"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Evidence favors the certainty of being caught over sheer severity of the penalty."},{"prompt":"Cesare Beccaria is best known for arguing that punishment should be...","options":["as harsh as possible","certain, swift, and proportionate","decided in secret","left to the victim"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Beccaria urged penalties that are prompt, proportionate, public, and defined by law in advance."},{"prompt":"Lombroso''s theory that criminals are a distinct physical type is now regarded as...","options":["the foundation of modern genetics","discredited and methodologically unsound","the basis of deterrence theory","a branch of victimology"],"answer":1,"explanation":"His physical typing of offenders was flawed and is rejected today, though his push for empirical study endured."},{"prompt":"Modern biosocial criminology emphasizes that offending results from...","options":["genes alone","environment alone","the interaction of biological and environmental factors","pure chance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Biosocial research stresses interaction - risk factors matter in combination with environment, not on their own."},{"prompt":"Which statement about crime data is most accurate?","options":["Police records capture nearly all crime","A rising recorded rate always means more offending","Different data sources should be cross-checked","Self-report studies are never useful"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because every source has blind spots, criminologists triangulate across police data, surveys, and self-reports."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Social structure theories: strain and social disorganization', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain social structure theories, which locate the roots of crime in the organization of society rather than in individuals. You will be able to describe strain theory and social disorganization theory, identify the pressures and places each highlights, and apply them to real patterns, such as why crime concentrates in some neighborhoods. Warm-up: if a society tells everyone to chase wealth but blocks many from legitimate paths to it, what might follow? By the end you should be able to distinguish a structural explanation from an individual one, and to see why these theories point toward policy that changes conditions - opportunity, investment, collective resources - rather than only punishing offenders. You will also be ready to weigh their limits, since structural pressures press on many people, most of whom never offend, so these theories explain rates and patterns better than they predict any single person''s behavior. Holding that distinction between group patterns and individual acts is a recurring skill in criminology."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Social structure theories argue that crime grows out of the arrangement of society, especially inequality and the uneven distribution of opportunity. Strain theory, developed by Robert Merton, begins with a mismatch: a culture urges everyone to pursue goals such as wealth and success, but the legitimate means to reach them are not equally available. People under this strain adapt in different ways; most conform, but some innovate by using illegitimate means, giving one route into crime. Later strain theorists broadened the idea to include many sources of stress and blocked goals. Social disorganization theory, from the Chicago School, shifts attention from individuals to places. It holds that neighborhoods marked by poverty, residential turnover, and instability struggle to maintain informal social control - the everyday supervision neighbors provide - which lets crime take root. In this view, high-crime areas stay high-crime as populations change, because the disorganization is a feature of the place, not the people. Both theories redirect attention from bad individuals toward the conditions that generate crime."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Robert Merton reframed Emile Durkheim''s concept of anomie, or normlessness, into strain theory in the 1930s, mapping five adaptations to the gap between goals and means: conformity, innovation, ritualism, retreatism, and rebellion. Innovation - pursuing culturally approved goals through disapproved means - is the adaptation most linked to crime. In the social disorganization tradition, Clifford Shaw and Henry McKay mapped crime across Chicago and found that certain zones kept high delinquency rates even as different groups moved through them, pointing to the neighborhood rather than any group as the source. Robert Sampson later refined this with the idea of collective efficacy: the willingness of neighbors to act for the common good, which predicts lower crime when it is strong. For a concrete example, two neighborhoods with similar poverty can differ sharply in crime if one has dense, trusting social ties and the other does not - evidence that social organization, not poverty by itself, is doing much of the work."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Try these, then check. 1) What mismatch sits at the heart of Merton''s strain theory? 2) Which of Merton''s adaptations is most associated with crime? 3) What does social disorganization theory say keeps some neighborhoods high in crime? 4) What is collective efficacy? 5) Why do these theories explain rates better than individual behavior? Answers: 1) A gap between culturally promoted goals, such as wealth, and the legitimate means available to reach them. 2) Innovation - using illegitimate means toward approved goals. 3) A weak capacity for informal social control, tied to poverty, turnover, and instability, which persists as populations change. 4) Neighbors'' shared willingness to intervene for the common good, which is linked to lower crime. 5) Because the same structural pressures affect many people, most of whom do not offend, so they shape patterns more than they determine any one choice."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reading strain theory as saying poverty directly causes crime - the claim is about blocked goals and adaptations, and most people under strain conform. Mistake: blaming the residents of high-crime neighborhoods, when disorganization theory locates the problem in conditions that follow the place, not the people. Mistake: using group-level patterns to predict a given individual. Exit ticket: (a) Who developed strain theory? (b) Fill in: strong ... efficacy predicts lower neighborhood crime. (c) True or false: social disorganization theory blames particular ethnic groups. Answers: (a) Robert Merton; (b) collective; (c) false - the same high-crime zones persisted across different groups, so the cause is the conditions of the place."}]}'::jsonb, 'These are structural explanations - they locate causes in the organization of society and opportunity, not in individual choice alone.', 13),
  (7, 'Social process theories: learning, control, and labeling', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain social process theories, which focus on how individuals come to offend through their interactions and relationships. You will be able to summarize differential association, social control (bonding) theory, and labeling theory, and say what each identifies as the key mechanism. Warm-up: if crime is learned like any other behavior, who does the teaching? By the end you should be able to take a life story and point to where each theory would look - the company a person keeps, the strength of their social bonds, or how officials have reacted to them. You will also be able to contrast these process accounts with the structural theories from the previous lesson, seeing structure and process as two levels of one picture: structures set the stage, processes play out in individual lives. A further skill is recognizing that these theories can complement one another, since learning, weak bonds, and labeling can all operate on the same person at once."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Social process theories explain offending through the everyday experiences that shape individuals. Differential association, from Edwin Sutherland, holds that criminal behavior is learned in interaction with others, especially close groups, where a person picks up not just techniques but attitudes favorable to breaking the law; when such definitions outweigh law-abiding ones, offending becomes more likely. Social control theory flips the usual question. Instead of asking why people offend, it asks why most people do not, and answers that strong bonds to society - attachment, commitment, involvement, and belief - restrain us, so crime rises when those bonds weaken. Labeling theory shifts focus to societal reaction: being publicly labeled a criminal can push a person toward further offending by reshaping their identity and limiting legitimate options, a process called secondary deviance. Together these theories trace crime through learning, bonding, and reaction. None claims to be the whole story, and each points to different levers, from the peers a young person associates with to how schools and courts respond to a first offense."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Edwin Sutherland set out differential association in the 1930s and 1940s, arguing crime is learned, not inherited, and emphasizing the ratio of pro-crime to anti-crime definitions a person absorbs. Travis Hirschi, in 1969, gave social control theory its influential form with four social bonds, showing that weak attachment and commitment predict delinquency. Howard Becker, a leading figure in labeling theory, argued that deviance is not a quality of the act but a consequence of the rules and sanctions applied to an offender - society creates deviance by labeling it. Edwin Lemert''s distinction between primary deviance (the initial act) and secondary deviance (offending that follows from being labeled) sharpened the theory. For an example, consider a teenager caught shoplifting once: control theory asks whether strong family and school bonds will pull them back; differential association asks whether their friends encourage or discourage further theft; labeling theory warns that a harsh official reaction could harden a passing act into an identity."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Attempt these, then check. 1) According to Sutherland, how is criminal behavior acquired? 2) What question does social control theory ask that others do not? 3) Name two of Hirschi''s four social bonds. 4) What does labeling theory mean by secondary deviance? 5) How might the three theories view the same first-time offender differently? Answers: 1) It is learned through interaction with others, especially close groups, including the attitudes that favor lawbreaking. 2) Why most people do not offend, rather than why some do. 3) Any two of attachment, commitment, involvement, and belief. 4) Further offending that results from being labeled deviant, as the label reshapes identity and opportunities. 5) Control theory checks the strength of their bonds; differential association checks the attitudes of their associates; labeling theory watches whether the official reaction pushes them toward a criminal identity."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reducing differential association to bad company alone - it is about learned attitudes and definitions, not the mere presence of offenders. Mistake: treating labeling theory as saying labels are the only cause of crime; it addresses how reaction can amplify offending, not how all crime begins. Mistake: assuming control theory ignores motivation; it simply treats restraint, not temptation, as the thing to explain. Exit ticket: (a) Who developed differential association? (b) Fill in: control theory explains crime by the weakening of social ... (c) True or false: labeling theory says the act itself defines deviance. Answers: (a) Edwin Sutherland; (b) bonds; (c) false - it says the reaction and the label, not the act alone, define deviance."}]}'::jsonb, 'Process theories ask how individuals become involved in crime over time, through learning, bonds, and reaction - not just where crime concentrates.', 14),
  (8, 'Critical and conflict criminology, and victimology', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain critical and conflict perspectives in criminology and introduce victimology. You will be able to summarize how conflict theory views law and the justice system, name a few critical strands, and describe what victimology studies and why it matters. Warm-up: who decides which harmful acts become crimes, and whose interests might that serve? By the end you should be able to take a law or an enforcement pattern and ask the conflict question - who benefits and who is burdened - while keeping the empirical discipline the field demands. You will also be able to explain why attention to victims, long neglected, reshaped both research and policy, from measuring victimization to improving how the system treats the harmed. The skill is to engage these critical arguments seriously as hypotheses about power and inequality that can be examined with evidence, rather than treating them as slogans, and to see victimology as completing a picture that once centered only on offenders."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Critical and conflict criminology question the assumption that law simply reflects a shared consensus about right and wrong. Drawing on Karl Marx and later thinkers, conflict theory argues that law and its enforcement often serve the interests of the powerful, so that similar harms are treated very differently depending on who commits them - street theft punished harshly, large-scale corporate harm lightly. Critical criminology is a broad family. Feminist criminology examines how gender shapes offending, victimization, and justice, correcting a field that had largely studied men. Other strands focus on race, class, and the reach of state power. These perspectives direct attention to how crime is defined and policed, not only to why individuals offend. Victimology, a related development, is the study of victims: who is victimized, the consequences they bear, their role in the justice process, and how systems can avoid harming them again. It gave rise to victimization surveys, victims'' rights, and restorative approaches that bring victims into responses to crime. Together these fields widen criminology beyond the offender."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Conflict criminology draws on Karl Marx, though Marx wrote little directly about crime; later scholars such as Willem Bonger connected economic conditions to offending. Feminist criminologists, including Carol Smart, challenged the field to take gender seriously, both in explaining crime and in studying how women experience justice as offenders and as victims. The study of white-collar crime, named by Edwin Sutherland, supports a core conflict claim: offenses by people of high status often cause enormous harm yet draw lighter responses than ordinary theft. In victimology, early work by Hans von Hentig and Benjamin Mendelsohn examined victim-offender relationships, though some early ideas about victim precipitation were rightly criticized for blaming victims. Modern victimology instead emphasizes needs, rights, and fair treatment. A concrete example is the spread of victim impact statements and restorative justice conferences, which give victims a voice - reforms that grew directly from taking victims seriously as a subject of study."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Try these, then check. 1) What central question does conflict theory ask about the law? 2) What gap did feminist criminology correct? 3) How does white-collar crime support a conflict argument? 4) What does victimology study? 5) Name one reform that grew out of victimology. Answers: 1) Whose interests the law and its enforcement serve, and whether they favor the powerful. 2) A field that had largely ignored gender and studied mainly men, both as offenders and as victims. 3) High-status offenders can cause large harm yet often face lighter responses than ordinary offenders, which suggests unequal treatment. 4) Victims - who is victimized, the harm they suffer, their role in the process, and how to avoid harming them again. 5) Victimization surveys, victims'' rights, victim impact statements, or restorative justice, among others."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating conflict theory as mere opinion rather than a set of claims that can be tested against patterns in lawmaking and enforcement. Mistake: the old victimology error of victim precipitation, which slid into blaming victims for the harm done to them. Mistake: assuming critical criminology rejects evidence; its strongest work is thoroughly empirical. Exit ticket: (a) Who named and studied white-collar crime? (b) Fill in: feminist criminology brought ... into the study of crime and justice. (c) True or false: modern victimology focuses on blaming victims. Answers: (a) Edwin Sutherland; (b) gender; (c) false - it focuses on victims'' needs, rights, and fair treatment."}]}'::jsonb, 'Conflict theories ask whose interests the law serves - a question about power and definition, not just about individual offenders.', 13),
  (9, 'Types of crime, the justice system, and crime prevention', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Classify the main types of crime and describe how the criminal justice system responds, including the debate between punishment and rehabilitation and the logic of crime prevention. You will be able to name categories such as violent, property, white-collar, organized, and cyber crime, outline the three stages of the justice system, and distinguish the main aims of sentencing. Warm-up: when someone is arrested, what three broad stages might they pass through? By the end you should be able to take a policy, such as a prevention program or a sentencing change, and reason about which aim it serves and what the evidence says. You will also be able to connect this lesson back to theory: how we define and count crimes, which explanations we find convincing, and how we treat victims all shape the system described here. The skill is to evaluate justice policy on evidence - does it reduce harm and reoffending - rather than on intuition alone."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Crimes are grouped in several ways. Violent crime involves force or its threat, such as assault and homicide; property crime, such as burglary and theft, targets possessions; white-collar crime involves deception for financial gain by people in positions of trust, such as fraud; organized crime refers to ongoing criminal enterprises; and cyber crime uses computers and networks. These categories overlap and shift as technology and law change. The criminal justice system that responds is usually described in three stages: police, who investigate and arrest; courts, which decide guilt and impose sentences; and corrections, which carry out penalties from fines to imprisonment to community supervision. At each stage, officials exercise discretion. Sentencing serves several, sometimes competing aims: retribution (deserved punishment), deterrence, incapacitation (preventing harm through confinement), and rehabilitation (reducing future offending by changing the person). The punishment-versus-rehabilitation debate weighs these aims. Crime prevention works earlier, trying to stop offenses before they occur, through social programs, situational design that reduces opportunity, and focused policing. Evidence increasingly supports prevention and rehabilitation alongside proportionate punishment."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"The three-stage model - police, courts, corrections - organizes most textbooks and real systems, though cases flow unevenly, with many dropping out before trial. On sentencing aims, retribution looks backward to desert, while deterrence, incapacitation, and rehabilitation look forward to future harm. Evidence shapes the debate: well-designed rehabilitation programs, following risk-need-responsivity principles, can modestly reduce reoffending, while incarceration alone often does not, and may worsen outcomes for low-risk offenders. Crime prevention offers concrete examples. Situational crime prevention, associated with Ronald Clarke, reduces opportunity through measures such as better lighting, locks, and surveillance, drawing on routine activity theory, which holds that crime needs a motivated offender, a suitable target, and the absence of a capable guardian. Developmental prevention invests early, for instance in quality early-childhood and parenting programs that show long-term reductions in offending. Focused deterrence programs concentrate enforcement and services on the small number of people and places driving most serious crime, with promising results in several evaluations."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Attempt these, then check. 1) Name four types of crime. 2) What are the three stages of the criminal justice system? 3) List the four common aims of sentencing. 4) According to routine activity theory, what three elements must converge for a crime? 5) What does the evidence suggest about rehabilitation versus incarceration alone? Answers: 1) Any four of violent, property, white-collar, organized, and cyber crime. 2) Police, courts, and corrections. 3) Retribution, deterrence, incapacitation, and rehabilitation. 4) A motivated offender, a suitable target, and the absence of a capable guardian. 5) Well-designed rehabilitation can modestly cut reoffending, while incarceration alone often does not reduce it and can worsen outcomes for low-risk offenders."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming the four aims of sentencing always agree; incapacitation and rehabilitation can pull in opposite directions. Mistake: treating prison as the only response, when fines, community supervision, and prevention are often more effective for the harm at hand. Mistake: thinking prevention means only more policing, when social and situational measures matter too. Exit ticket: (a) Which sentencing aim looks backward to deserved punishment? (b) Name the three elements in routine activity theory. (c) True or false: longer incarceration reliably rehabilitates low-risk offenders. Answers: (a) retribution; (b) a motivated offender, a suitable target, and the absence of a capable guardian; (c) false - for low-risk offenders, incarceration can worsen rather than improve outcomes."}]}'::jsonb, 'The justice system is usually described as three linked stages - police, courts, and corrections - each with its own discretion and limits.', 15),
  (10, 'Key terms', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"criminology","translation":"the scientific study of crime, offenders, and society''s response to crime","example":"Criminology draws on sociology, law, psychology, and statistics to explain patterns of offending."},{"term":"dark figure of crime","translation":"the volume of crime that occurs but never appears in official records","example":"Because many thefts go unreported, the dark figure of crime keeps police data below the true total."},{"term":"deterrence","translation":"preventing crime by making its expected costs outweigh its rewards","example":"Research suggests the certainty of being caught drives deterrence more than the severity of the sentence."},{"term":"anomie","translation":"a condition of weak or unclear social norms, linked by Merton to strain","example":"Durkheim used anomie to describe the normlessness that can accompany rapid social change."},{"term":"differential association","translation":"Sutherland''s theory that criminal behavior is learned through interaction with others","example":"Differential association explains offending by the attitudes a person absorbs from close groups."},{"term":"labeling theory","translation":"the view that being officially defined as deviant can push a person toward further offending","example":"Under labeling theory, a harsh reaction to a first offense can harden it into an identity."},{"term":"recidivism","translation":"a return to offending after punishment or treatment, also called reoffending","example":"Programs are judged partly by whether they lower recidivism among released offenders."},{"term":"victimology","translation":"the study of victims, their experiences, needs, and role in the justice process","example":"Victimology shaped reforms such as victim impact statements and restorative justice."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-000000000028' and l.position = v.pos
